          "fontSize": 11.5,
          "fontFamily": 1,
          "text": "We deliver large datasets to enterprise customers. Today a\ncustomer receives a folder of files plus a spreadsheet describing\nwhat is inside.\n\nWe want to replace that with a customer-facing portal. A customer\nshould be able to:\n1. see the datasets delivered to them;\n2. open a dataset, filter and browse its datapoints, and inspect\none datapoint;\n3. download the full dataset or a filtered subset in a useful\nformat; and\n4. formally accept a dataset delivery.\n\nA dataset typically contains 1–10 million datapoints. A datapoint is\none unit of content—or a reference to a large file—plus\nstructured metadata and basic provenance. The content may be\ntext, an image, audio, or a conversation. Metadata is a set of\nnamed values such as language, sentiment, category, quality score,\nor modality-specific annotations.\n\nBefore designing, ask whatever you need to understand the user,\nthe requirements, and what you would put in or out of scope. You\ncan make reasonable assumptions if you state them.",
          "textAlign": "left",
          "verticalAlign": "top",
          "containerId": null,
          "originalText": "We deliver large datasets to enterprise customers. Today a customer receives a folder of files plus a spreadsheet describing what is inside.\n\nWe want to replace that with a customer-facing portal. A customer should be able to:\n1. see the datasets delivered to them;\n2. open a dataset, filter and browse its datapoints, and inspect one datapoint;\n3. download the full dataset or a filtered subset in a useful format; and\n4. formally accept a dataset delivery.\n\nA dataset typically contains 1–10 million datapoints. A datapoint is one unit of content—or a reference to a large file—plus structured metadata and basic provenance. The content may be text, an image, audio, or a conversation. Metadata is a set of named values such as language, sentiment, category, quality score, or modality-specific annotations.\n\nBefore designing, ask whatever you need to understand the user, the requirements, and what you would put in or out of scope. You can make reasonable assumptions if you state them.",
